In Spring Boot, both @Primary and @Qualifier are used to resolve dependency injection ambiguity. When you have multiple beans implementing the same interface or sharing the same parent type, Spring will throw a NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException because it does not know which bean to choose.


Direct Comparison


Feature	        @Primary	                                                          @Qualifier
Purpose:	    Defines a default fallback bean.	                         Explicitly selects a specific bean by name.
Placement:	    Source level: Placed on the bean configuration or class.	 Target level: Placed at the injection point.
Flexibility:    Set once; applies automatically unless overridden.	         Configured manually at every specific point you need it.
Precedence:	    Lower priority.	                                             Higher priority (overrides @Primary).




1. @Primary (The Default Choice)

You use @Primary when you have a preferred implementation that should be used most of the time.
java
public interface NotificationService {
    void send(String message);
}

@Component
@Primary // Marks this as the default bean
public class EmailNotification implements NotificationService {
    public void send(String message) { System.out.println("Email: " + message); }
}

@Component
public class SmsNotification implements NotificationService {
    public void send(String message) { System.out.println("SMS: " + message); }
}
Use code with caution.
Injection Point:
java
@RestController
public class NotificationController {
    @Autowired
    private NotificationService notificationService;
    // Spring automatically injects EmailNotification because it is marked @Primary
}





2. @Qualifier (The Specific Choice)

You use @Qualifier when you need exact control over which bean gets injected at a particular location. It matches beans based on their names (which default to camelCase of the class name).
Injection Point:
java
@RestController
public class UrgentNotificationController {
    private final NotificationService notificationService;

    // Tells Spring to ignore defaults and specifically fetch the "smsNotification" bean
    @Autowired
    public UrgentNotificationController(@Qualifier("smsNotification") NotificationService notificationService) {
        this.notificationService = notificationService;
    }
}

Core Mechanics: What happens if you use both?

If a bean is marked @Primary but you explicitly request a different bean using @Qualifier at the injection point, @Qualifier always wins.

